\path (4,2) node[right] {$B$};
\path (3,4) node[above] {$C$};
\path (-1,2) node[left] {$D$};
\path (1.5,2) node[above left] {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Quadrilateral $ABCD$ has vertices $A(0,0)$, $B(3,1)$, $C(2,4)$ and $D(-1,3)$. Show that it is a square using:

a. opposite-side gradients, an adjacent right angle and adjacent side lengths;

b. diagonal lengths, gradients and midpoints;

c. all four side lengths and the diagonal lengths.

examples.solution_text:
a.

$\begin{aligned}m_{AB}&=\frac{1-0}{3-0}=\frac13\\m_{DC}&=\frac{4-3}{2-(-1)}=\frac13\\m_{BC}&=\frac{4-1}{2-3}=-3\\m_{AD}&=\frac{3-0}{-1-0}=-3\end{aligned}$

Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel, so $ABCD$ is a parallelogram. Since $m_{AB}m_{BC}=\frac13(-3)=-1$, it is a rectangle.

$\begin{aligned}AB&=\sqrt{(3-0)^2+(1-0)^2}=\sqrt{10}\\BC&=\sqrt{(2-3)^2+(4-1)^2}=\sqrt{10}\end{aligned}$

A rectangle with equal adjacent sides is a square. Its opposite sides are equal, so all four sides equal $\sqrt{10}$.

b.

$\begin{aligned}AC&=\sqrt{(2-0)^2+(4-0)^2}=\sqrt{20}\\BD&=\sqrt{(-1-3)^2+(3-1)^2}=\sqrt{20}\\m_{AC}&=\frac{4-0}{2-0}=2\\m_{BD}&=\frac{3-1}{-1-3}=-\frac12\\M_{AC}&=\left(\frac{0+2}{2},\frac{0+4}{2}\right)=(1,2)\\M_{BD}&=\left(\frac{3+(-1)}{2},\frac{1+3}{2}\right)=(1,2)\end{aligned}$

$m_{AC}m_{BD}=2\left(-\frac12\right)=-1$. The diagonals are equal, perpendicular and bisect each other, so $ABCD$ is a square.

c. Calculate the remaining side lengths directly.

$\begin{aligned}CD&=\sqrt{(-1-2)^2+(3-4)^2}=\sqrt{10}\\DA&=\sqrt{(0-(-1))^2+(0-3)^2}=\sqrt{10}\end{aligned}$

$AB=BC=CD=DA=\sqrt{10}$, so $ABCD$ is a rhombus. Also, $AC=BD=\sqrt{20}$. A rhombus with equal diagonals is a square. This proof uses lengths alone.

